WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.000
Your cells build p53 all day long and shred half of each new batch within about

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.120
twenty minutes. Why make a guardian only to destroy it?

3
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.800
Because an emergency cannot wait for a gene to be read and a protein built.

4
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.840
When the responder already exists, the cell only has to let go.

5
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:30.043
p53 is a transcription factor, a protein that binds DNA to turn genes on.

6
00:00:31.243 --> 00:00:36.403
In a calm cell, a partner called MDM2 grips it and covers its active end.

7
00:00:38.283 --> 00:00:43.743
MDM2 also tags it with ubiquitin, a mark sending proteins to the proteasome

8
00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:44.743
shredder.

9
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:47.163
Why does steady shredding give speed?

10
00:00:48.443 --> 00:00:53.603
At balance, when making equals destroying, protein level equals synthesis rate

11
00:00:53.603 --> 00:00:55.843
divided by the destruction rate constant.

12
00:00:57.143 --> 00:01:02.663
Suppose synthesis adds 80 units an hour, and the destruction constant is 4 per hour.

13
00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:06.783
80 ÷ 4 = 20 units at rest.

14
00:01:08.123 --> 00:01:14.323
Cut the constant fourfold, to 1, and 80 ÷ 1 = 80 units, with no new gene read.

15
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.257
Now radiation snaps both strands of DNA.

16
00:01:19.577 --> 00:01:24.697
That break activates a kinase called ATM, an enzyme that adds phosphate tags.

17
00:01:25.977 --> 00:01:31.177
ATM and downstream kinases tag both MDM2 and p53.

18
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:39.797
MDM2's grip loosens, destruction drops, and p53 climbs fast, switching on genes that

19
00:01:39.797 --> 00:01:41.377
pause division and start repair.

20
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.600
Many stress pathways hold responders back until stress removes the restraint.

21
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.920
Broken DNA has a dedicated kinase sensor.

22
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.000
Now imagine dozens of unrelated toxic chemicals entering the cell instead.

23
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.480
How could one protein detect an entire chemical class without a custom pocket for

24
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.480
each toxin? Pause and predict.

25
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.493
The sensor works by being attacked.

26
00:02:16.913 --> 00:02:21.173
Many chemical threats are electrophiles, molecules hungry for electrons.

27
00:02:22.573 --> 00:02:27.633
Their responder, Nrf2, is made all day and turns on protective enzymes.

28
00:02:28.953 --> 00:02:32.553
A partner called Keap1 normally passes it to the shredder.

29
00:02:33.813 --> 00:02:38.773
Keap1 carries cysteines whose sulfur atoms give up electrons easily, so

30
00:02:38.773 --> 00:02:40.413
electrophiles react with them.

31
00:02:40.413 --> 00:02:43.613
It reads electron hunger, not shape.

32
00:02:44.617 --> 00:02:49.697
On the current view, altered Keap1 stops passing Nrf2 for destruction.

33
00:02:50.977 --> 00:02:55.537
With the catchers tied up, newly made Nrf2 escapes straight to the nucleus.

34
00:02:56.957 --> 00:03:02.397
Other pathways reuse this trick, holding intact kinases or folding sensors quiet

35
00:03:02.397 --> 00:03:03.617
until stress arrives.

36
00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.837
Put both systems side by side.

37
00:03:08.077 --> 00:03:12.537
Building responders ahead costs energy, but buys minutes when danger strikes.

38
00:03:13.937 --> 00:03:17.557
Lifting a ready restraint lets the cell answer stress right away.
